  <p>Does therapy actually move the needle on depression? Fair to ask before you spend your energy on it. The approaches we lean on, solution-focused work, CBT, ACT, and DBT, are among the most studied in the field, and CBT for depression in particular carries decades of meta-analytic support (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3584580/">Hofmann and colleagues' 2012 review</a> is the standard reference). An average from a study is not a promise about your particular recovery. It just means this path has been walked before, by a lot of people who also weren't sure it would work.</p>
  <p>And the ocean a few blocks away isn't only scenery. In <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-44097-3">a study of nearly 20,000 people</a>, spending at least two hours a week in natural settings was associated with noticeably better health and well-being (White et al., 2019, <em>Scientific Reports</em>). For a condition that shrinks your world, a reason to step back into it is worth something.</p>
{% endcall %}
